    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="background">Ones intuition is defined as the act or faculty of knowing or sensing without the use of rational processes; immediate cognition (yourdictionary.com).</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="22" relname="span">As computing evolved from the black and white text-based DOS environment to the colorful world of Graphical User Interface,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="24" relname="span">many applications have been praised to be natural, user-friendly, and intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="21" relname="span">However, the mere fact that an environment is GUI based does not mean that it is intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="23" relname="span">In many cases the term intuitiveness has been used in scenarios where it took time to develop some sense or knowledge of the system.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="nonvolitional-result">Consequently, this is contrary to the true meaning of the word intuitive, which is having immediate cognition.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="25" relname="span">Branding a product as being intuitive may force developers to oversimplify the product or even prevent further advancement;</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="nonvolitional-cause">since any newly added feature might not be intuitive because it is not familiar (Raskin, 1994).</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="27" relname="concession">Though the term has been used as a gimmick by marketers,</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="27" relname="span">I think it can be a useful concept for designers in releasing systems that would be used by people with all levels of experience.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="28" relname="span">It is important to measure intuitiveness when introducing a totally new system</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="30" relname="conjunction">where time and satisfaction is a concern,</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="30" relname="conjunction">and where the user of the new system accomplished the same task before by a completely different method.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="32" relname="span">Examples of these systems include automated check-in machines at airports, and self-checkout cashiers machine at stores.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="31" relname="span">Here the first encounter with the user must be a success</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="15" relname="antithesis">or else the implementation of the system is a failure.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="18" relname="joint">Intuitiveness can be measured in a control environment by recording completion times of task and tracking how many times users ask for help.</segment>
        <group id="18" type="multinuc"/>
        <group id="19" type="span" parent="18" relname="joint"/>
